SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain is blinding, but I have to speak very quietly and calmly, otherwise the hospital will just tell me I'm hysterical and send me home with nothing again.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a history of profound clinical exhaustion from years of undiagnosed chronic pain (suspected endometriosis/adenomyosis). Over the past 24 hours, the primary gynecological condition severely deteriorated due to delayed care, resulting in agonizing, unremitting pelvic pain, vomiting, and near-syncope. The patient exhibits severe internalized self-doubt regarding the objective reality of the pain due to repeated instances of severe physical symptoms being misdiagnosed as anxiety or hysteria. The patient demonstrates performative calmness, speaking in an artificially flat, quiet tone despite being in obvious agony, feeling forced to perform this demeanor to be taken seriously by providers. The partner provides a defensive and heavily documented communication style, presenting a large binder of past medical encounters to force medical intervention and avoid being labeled drug-seeking.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound, writhing in pain, unable to tolerate oral fluids or stand upright.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of preventable severe morbidity (e.g., ovarian torsion, ruptured cyst) due to the complete avoidance of emergency care driven by profound institutional betrayal.

