CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, agonizing pelvic pain, reactive tachycardia (HR 115), and marked abdominal guarding in the context of extreme medical trauma and care avoidance. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely essential to perform a targeted, respectful abdominal examination to rule out an immediate surgical emergency, and to administer the potent, injectable analgesia that had been systemically and inappropriately withheld in the past. Delaying assessment or forcing an ED transfer would have resulted in complete care refusal, posing an imminent risk of severe morbidity, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and intramuscular prochlorperazine 12.5mg to immediately halt the severe pain and vomiting. Wrote a forceful, objective clinical handover letter detailing the physical findings to bypass ED triage bias if symptoms worsen. Advised the usual GP to conduct a chart review showing years of negative psychosomatic diagnoses and expedite an urgent, comprehensive pelvic MRI/ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe pain flares while awaiting specialist care, utilize a "layered analgesia protocol": set an alarm to alternate paracetamol and ibuprofen strictly every 3 hours round-the-clock during a flare, rather than waiting for the pain to peak; staying ahead of the pain curve mechanically reduces somatic hyperalgesia. To overcome the need for performative calmness, utilize the partner as a "designated symptom reporter" during consultations; allowing the partner to present the objective data frees the patient to express their pain naturally without fear of being labeled hysterical.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized, trauma-informed endometriosis/pelvic pain clinic and a patient advocacy service.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to monitor the efficacy of the injection, review the clinical handover, and expedite formal diagnostic imaging.

